12:27:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely understand why you’re lost. Let me put it in the simplest terms I can, because your logic actually makes sense: *if the device is connected to the internet, why wouldn’t it just get whatever update exists?* The missing piece is that **being connected is only the road. It’s not the destination. The update has to be *sent* down that road, and if nobody sends it, nothing arrives.** ### The mailbox analogy Think of your device’s internet connection like a mailbox at the end of your driveway. It’s physically there, the post office knows where it is, and every day the mail carrier could stop by. But whether a letter actually ends up inside your mailbox depends entirely on whether someone *wrote you a letter and mailed it*. - Your uncle’s 10‑year‑old TV still gets firmware updates because the manufacturer still writes those letters every year and puts them in the mail for that TV’s address. - An old phone that stops getting major OS updates is like a person who nobody writes to anymore. The mailbox is still there, the connection works perfectly, but there’s simply **no letter being sent to that address**. The manufacturer stopped creating new updates for that phone model, so there’s nothing for the phone to download, even if it checks every five minutes. So when your phone says “Your software is up to date,” it’s not lying. It checked the mothership, and the mothership said, “I have nothing newer for you. You’re on the latest version I ever built for your model.” The internet connection worked perfectly—the device just got a reply that said, “no new mail.” ### Why would a company stop writing those letters? Because building a new version of iOS or Android for a 7‑ or 10‑year‑old phone’s hardware is an enormous, expensive engineering task. It’s not as simple as just flipping a switch. The OS has to be tested on that exact chipset, with those exact cameras, cellular radios, etc. At some point, companies decide it’s not worth the cost, especially when very few people still use that model. So they stop creating new updates for it. **The phone is still perfectly connected to the internet; the update simply never gets created.** ### But what about that ancient TV? That’s the exception that proves the rule. The TV’s firmware is tiny and simple compared to a full smartphone OS. It probably shares its core platform with a dozen other TV models sold over many years. So the manufacturer finds it cheap and easy to keep sending those little letters—often just security patches or minor bug fixes—and they do it for a decade because people keep TVs that long and might sue them if a vulnerability makes the news. The TV’s mailbox is still getting mail because it’s still worth mailing, and the postage is cheap. ### Bringing it back to your device When I used the word “eligible,” I only meant “has a mailbox that still gets mail from the sender.” Your device being turned on and connected means the mailbox flag is up. But if the sender stopped writing, the flag alone doesn’t create a letter. That’s why an old phone—even one that’s online every day—won’t ever receive a shiny new Android 15 update. The mailbox is ready, but there’s simply no letter addressed to it. So no, the whole point of being connected isn’t to *guarantee* you’ll get every update forever. It’s to *enable* you to get any update that the manufacturer happens to release for your specific device. When the manufacturer stops releasing them, the connection sits there, perfectly functional, but empty. Your uncle’s TV is still getting mail because the manufacturer still finds value in sending it. Your old phone eventually doesn’t, and that’s the difference.
